Metabolite identification from HNMR spectroscopy traditionally requires expert interpretation and manual database searching. This project developed a machine learning pipeline to automatically classify 847 metabolite compounds from the Human Metabolome Database into five chemical groups based on HNMR peak lists. Spectra were preprocessed by binning chemical shifts into 400 bins (0.025 ppm resolution, 0-10 ppm range) with log transformation and normalization.
Three modeling approaches were evaluated: two convolutional neural network architectures and Random Forest classifiers. Both CNNs failed dramatically (15-23% test accuracy) with severe overfitting, while Random Forest achieved 66.4% test accuracy after hyperparameter tuning. Principal Component Analysis revealed that only 12% of variance was captured by the first three components, explaining why the sparse, high-dimensional data with limited samples favored ensemble methods over deep learning.
Feature importance and SHAP analysis demonstrated chemically interpretable patterns: aliphatic regions (1.2-1.6 ppm) discriminated Lipids (86% recall), aromatic signals (6-8 ppm) identified Aromatics (68% recall), and alpha-to-heteroatom resonances (3.6 ppm) distinguished nitrogen- and oxygen-containing compounds. A Streamlit web application was deployed for real-time classification, successfully validating on external data by classifying a testosterone spectrum from a different database with 75% confidence. This work demonstrates that machine learning can extract meaningful chemical patterns from NMR data, though the 66% accuracy ceiling indicates the approach is best suited as a decision-support tool rather than fully autonomous identifier.